  {
    "key": "flow.model",
    "value": "trunk",
    "source": "default",
    "doc": "'trunk' (default): every task branches from and lands on one base branch \u2014 today's behaviour. 'gitflow': tasks branch from `develop_branch` as feature/ or bugfix/ branches, hotfixes branch from and land on `production_branch` and are back-merged into develop, and `version cut` makes a release/ branch, merges it to production, tags it and back-merges it. An unrecognised value in a config file is warned about, reported by `doctor` and falls back to 'trunk', the strictest (no safety dimension; the plain model, which moves no branches); `config --set`, `ddflow_configure` and the environment refuse it, and so does loading ddflow's own source tree, where the file and the code are one commit."
  },
  {
    "key": "flow.integration",
    "value": "merge",
    "source": "default",
    "doc": "'merge' (default): `ddflow merge` lands the branch locally. 'pr': it pushes the branch and opens (or updates) a pull/merge request instead, releases the lease and parks the item in REVIEW so the agent can take the next task; `ddflow pr sync` completes it when the forge says it merged. Use 'pr' wherever merges need approval. An unrecognised value in a config file is warned about, reported by `doctor` and falls back to 'merge', the strictest (a typo never pushes or opens a pull request; lands locally); `config --set`, `ddflow_configure` and the environment refuse it, and so does loading ddflow's own source tree, where the file and the code are one commit."
